<section>
  <h2>Call site</h2>
  <code lang=cpp id=flow>
    int main() {
      auto result = process("42", 2);

      if (result) {
        std::cout << "Result: " << *result << "\n";
      } else {
        std::cout << "Error: " << result.error() << "\n";
      }

      return 0;
    }
  </code>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Summary</h2>

  <p>
    <c>and_then</c> is <c>>>=</c> / <c>flatMap</c>. <c>transform</c> is <c>fmap</c>/<c>map</c>. <c>std::expected</c>
    is <c>Either</c>, <c>std::optional</c> is <c>Maybe</c>. C++23 finally has what Haskell
    has had all along -- just under different names.
  </p>

  <p>Thanks!</p>
</section>

<notes>
  Ask for questions
</notes>

<notes>
  If time permits: mention that std::expected has the monadic API right away, no need to wait for another standard
</notes>
